  "payload": {
    "rawResponseText": "When a hotel owner in the Caribbean and Latin America (CALA) considers a hotel conversion using a **collection or \"soft-brand\" model**, they are typically looking for the best of both worlds: maintaining the property’s unique identity, local architecture, and independent charm while tapping into a global reservation system, lower OTA commissions, and a major loyalty program.\n\nBecause nearly 70% of lodging supply in CALA is independent, major global chains and independent networks actively compete for these conversions. The choice depends on the property's scale, market tier, capital budget (PIP requirements), and target demographic.\n\nBelow are the most commonly considered soft-brand options in the region, categorized by global parent company and market tier.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Marriott International\nMarriott leads in conversion market share across CALA, backed by the distribution power of Marriott Bonvoy.\n*   **Autograph Collection (Upper Upscale / Luxury):** One of the dominant players in CALA for upscale boutique and resort conversions. It covers both European-plan (EP) properties and All-Inclusive resorts (often partnered with Sunwing/Blue Diamond).\n*   **Tribute Portfolio (Upper Upscale / Lifestyle):** Targeted at boutique, design-forward, or indie-vibe properties in both primary cities (e.g., Bogotá, Mexico City) and coastal markets.\n*   **The Luxury Collection (Luxury):** Reserved for iconic, historic, or landmark properties with exceptional service standards (e.g., *Hacienda Temozon* in Mexico, *Tambodel Inka* in Peru).\n*   **Design Hotels:** Highly curated for avant-garde, architecture-focused boutique properties.\n\n### 2. Hilton\nHilton’s soft brands have expanded rapidly across CALA, heavily relying on the reach of the Hilton Honors loyalty network.\n*   **Curio Collection by Hilton (Upper Upscale / Luxury):** A premier option for unique resort properties and boutique hotels in Central/South America and the Caribbean (e.g., *Mahogany Bay Resort* in Belize, *Zemi Miches* in Dominican Republic).\n*   **Tapestry Collection by Hilton (Upscale):** Geared toward mid-to-upper-tier indie hotels with vibrant local roots, offering a lower capital entry point than Curio.\n*   **Small Luxury Hotels of the World (SLH) Partnership:** Hilton’s strategic distribution alliance with SLH allows independent luxury boutique hotels in CALA to plug into Hilton’s distribution system without flying a traditional franchise flag.\n\n### 3. Hyatt Hotels Corporation\nHyatt’s footprint in CALA has expanded significantly, particularly following its acquisition of Apple Leisure Group (ALG) and expansion into lifestyle and all-inclusive spaces.\n*   **The Unbound Collection by Hyatt (Luxury / Upper Upscale):** Best for high-end properties with rich history or distinctive stories.\n*   **Destination by Hyatt (Upscale / Luxury Resorts):** Focuses on leisure resorts, residential resorts, and golf/spa properties that celebrate their specific destination.\n*   **JdV by Hyatt (Upscale / Lifestyle):** Neighborhood-centric, vibrant lifestyle properties in urban and beach destinations.\n*   **Hyatt Inclusive Collection:** Though primarily hard-flagged (Secrets, Dreams, Zoëtry), Hyatt frequently converts existing Caribbean all-inclusive resorts into these specialized brands.\n\n### 4. IHG Hotels & Resorts\n*   **Vignette Collection (Luxury / Lifestyle):** IHG’s newest luxury conversion brand, actively targeting boutique coastal resorts and historic urban conversions in CALA. It allows owners to keep their property name while joining the IHG One Rewards system.\n\n### 5. Accor\nAccor has a strong regional footprint in South America (especially Brazil, Colombia, and Argentina) and the French West Indies.\n*   **MGallery Hotel Collection (Upper Upscale):** Focuses on boutique hotels with strong storytelling, heritage, or boutique luxury aesthetics (e.g., *Santa Marta Hotel* in Colombia).\n*   **Handwritten Collection (Midscale / Upscale):** Accor’s flexible soft brand designed for smaller, character-rich independent hotels seeking quick conversion.\n*   **Emblems Collection (Luxury):** Intended for top-tier heritage and luxury boutique estates.\n\n### 6. Wyndham Hotels & Resorts\n*   **Registry Collection Hotels (Luxury / Resort):** Built specifically for luxury resort conversions, high-end condo-hotels, and timeshare/fractional properties in coastal markets (e.g., Mexico, Dominican Republic).\n*   **Trademark Collection by Wyndham (Upper Midscale / Upscale):** A popular choice for mid-market independent owners looking for cost-effective conversions without heavy structural PIP burdens.\n\n### 7. Choice Hotels International\n*   **Ascend Hotel Collection (Upscale):** One of the oldest soft brands in the industry. It is widely used in Caribbean island markets (e.g., Sint Maarten, Bahamas) for small-to-mid-sized independent beachfront hotels and boutique resorts.\n\n---\n\n### Non-Franchise / Independent Alliances (Soft Representation)\nOwners who want zero operational interference from major chain standards often opt for marketing consortia/representation brands instead of a traditional franchise:\n*   **Preferred Hotels & Resorts** (Legend, LVX, Connect, Preferred Residences) – Popular in Mexico and resort destinations.\n*   **The Leading Hotels of the World (LHW)** – Standard choice for ultra-luxury, owner-operated grand hotels.\n*   **Relais & Châteaux** – Ideal for small, food-and-beverage-driven luxury boutique estates.\n\n---\n\n### Key Decision Drivers for CALA Owners\n\n| Factor | Primary Considerations in CALA |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **All-Inclusive Compatibility** | Does the soft brand support All-Inclusive operations? (*Autograph AI, Hyatt Inclusive Collection, Registry Collection excel here*). |\n| **PIP Costs (Conversion CapEx)** | Soft brands require less physical standardisation than hard flags, but Luxury soft brands (Unbound, Luxury Collection) still carry high CapEx requirements. |\n| **Loyalty Program Penetration** | North American feeder markets dominate CALA tourism; access to Bonvoy, Hilton Honors, or World of Hyatt drives high-margin direct bookings. |\n| **OTA Dependence** | CALA independent hotels often suffer from high OTA commissions (20%+); soft-brand CRS/Direct channels lower guest acquisition costs. |",
